# Literal proprietary terms this repository must never publish — one per line.
#
# `make install-hooks` copies this template to <git-common-dir>/proprietary-terms on
# first run, and never overwrites it afterwards. That location is deliberate: git never
# commits, pushes, or clones anything inside .git/, so the terms cannot leak through the
# very repository they protect. Do not move the live list into the working tree.
#
# THIS FILE STARTS WITH NO TERMS, so literal-term checking is OFF until you add some.
# The hook says so on every run while that is true. The structural pattern still works,
# but it cannot see a hostname — only a term here can.
#
# Matching is fixed-string (never a regex) and case-insensitive, and is applied per
# line, so a term broken across a line break is not detected.
#
# COMMENTS: a '#' opens a comment only when followed by a space or end of line. So
# `#acme-secret` is a TERM (an internal channel or tag), while `# a note` is a comment
# and `acme-corp   # a note` is a term with a trailing comment. A term may contain
# spaces and is matched as a whole phrase, not as its separate words.
#
# Add an entry for anything that identifies an employer, client, colleague, or internal
# system:
#
#   gitlab.internal.example      # internal hostnames
#   acme-corp                    # company or group names
#   acme-base/telemetry          # internal project namespaces
#   Some Client Ltd              # client names
#   #acme-private-channel        # a term that itself begins with '#'
#
# The hook also enforces one structural pattern that needs no entry here:
# /home/<user>/work/ paths, whose shape identifies work context without naming anyone.
# Personal paths such as /home/<user>/projects/ are not flagged.
